10/08/2026

Inspected dataset. Fixations directiory contains '.mat' files. These files contain info about 
each data point. In particular, they have the following fields:
-image
-resolution
-gaze: which contains the truth gaze data from subject (each structure corresponds to one 
	subject)
	-location: this is the image location of the raw data gaze points [x,y] 
	-timestamp: the timestamp (ms) of each raw gaze point
	-fixations: fixations points [x,y]
These are training and validation data, while testing contains only image and resolution.
From these data basically i should be able to reconstruct the trajectory of the subject while
observing the image (this for each subject). Each point is a pair [x,y] and the timestamp is
the moment at which the subject observes that point. From what i have understood, these points
are not obtaine with an eye tracker, but with something else called mouse (idk whether is a 
pointer or whatever). Therefore location[i] + timestamp[i] = trajectory. Instead, fixations 
contains points indicated as fixations points obtained somehow. Therefore, for each image,
i have the fixations from different subjects (each image has a different number of subjects that
seems to span from ~45 to ~70.

Idk what to do and whether these data will be useful but ftm this is what i know. Maybe i can
use them for reconstructing the ground-truth maps or use them as validation metrics.	

